                <text x="20" y="73" class="m" font-size="8.5" fill="var(--mut)">28 sep</text>
                <text x="66" y="73" class="m" font-size="9" fill="var(--ok)">haiku-4.5 &rarr; mistral-small</text>
                <text x="298" y="73" text-anchor="end" class="m" font-size="9" font-weight="600" fill="var(--ok)">-$33 / mo</text>
              </g>
              <g>
                <rect x="8" y="88" width="302" height="26" rx="7" fill="var(--panel)" stroke="var(--line)" stroke-width="1.2"></rect>
                <text x="20" y="105" class="m" font-size="8.5" fill="var(--mut)">04 oct</text>
                <text x="66" y="105" class="m" font-size="9" fill="var(--mut)">checked, nothing cheaper passed</text>
                <text x="298" y="105" text-anchor="end" class="m" font-size="9" fill="var(--mut)">no change</text>
              </g>
            </svg>
          </div>
          <div class="meta"><div class="nm"><span class="tag new">P</span>The switch log</div><div class="wh">A log rather than a chart, ending on the check that found nothing cheaper, which is what most entries are.</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="font-size: 13px; color: var(--mut-read); line-height: 1.6; margin: 20px 0 0; max-width: 84ch;">
        All six say the same thing and differ in where the eye lands. The bars and the running total land on the
        amount, the gap lands on the comparison with what you were paying, and the check strip lands on the part
        that is hardest to say in a chart, which is that it never stops. The log is the only one that names models.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- ============================================================ the panel -->
